// Secure Device Enrollment Protocol (SDEP) — Attacker-visible gaps.
//
// This module mechanises security obligations the protocol's decision
// logic DOES NOT meet. Every `property` here is expected to FAIL when
// proved with Z3: the counterexample is the concrete attack.
//
// The purpose of the file is NOT to ship a working defence, it is to
// keep a regression list of known holes so they can be triaged or
// fixed without forgetting one. `prove_gaps.ps1` runs them and PASSES
// when every property fails with a counterexample; if a future change
// closes a gap, the corresponding property starts proving Q.E.D. and
// the script flips the entry to "FIXED — close the gap row in SPEC.md".
//
// Mapping to spec.md §6 "Known Gaps":
//
//   G30  Provision has no caller authentication           §6.1
//   G31  Provision response code is an active-key oracle  §6.2
//   G32  getStatus reveals state to unauthenticated calls §6.3
//   G33  Activation response code is a keyId oracle       §6.4
//   G34  Claims are checked by name, not by value         §6.5
//   G35  HMAC accepts a zero/low-entropy key              §6.6
//
// Stateful gaps that are NOT Cryptol-expressible (documented only):
//
//   G36  No nonce / no replay-window state                §6.7
//   G37  Provision endpoint has no method check           §6.8
//   G38  constant_time_equals is not control-flow proven  §6.9
//
// The file imports SDEP so the "secure" reference functions can be
// compared to the actual production decision functions directly.

module SDEP_gaps where

import SDEP

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// G30 — Provision has no caller authentication.
//
// `provisionKey` takes `validRequest` (a flag computed from headers
// only — X-Fleet-Metadata, X-Fleet-Auth-Scheme), `vaultResult`, and
// `keyIsActive`. There is no AuthResult input. The C++ controller's
// `handle_provision` likewise never inspects a signature or claims for
// POST /enroll/key. Anyone on the link-local network (the spec's only
// transport restriction) can drive the key store from "no key" to
// "provisional" with attacker-controlled secret material — then race
// the device to activate. The attacker becomes the device.
//
// `secureProvisionKey` below is what the function SHOULD have been:
// the same control flow, plus an AuthResult input that must be
// Authenticated before any state change. G30 asserts the two agree on
// every well-formed input; Z3 exhibits a counterexample on
// `authResult = Unauthenticated`, which is the attack.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

secureProvisionKey :
  Bit -> Bit -> KeyVaultResult -> Bit -> AuthResult -> ProvisionResult
secureProvisionKey fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult keyIsActive authResult =
  if ~ fleetEnabled                  then PR_Disabled
   | ~ validRequest                  then PR_BadRequest
   | authResult != AR_Authenticated  then PR_Unauthorized
   | vaultResult != KV_Ok            then PR_InternalError
   | keyIsActive                     then PR_Unauthorized
  else                                    PR_Succeeded

// EXPECTED VERDICT: FAIL.
// Counterexample shape: fleetEnabled = True, validRequest = True,
// vaultResult = KV_Ok, keyIsActive = False, authResult = AR_Unauthenticated.
// LHS = PR_Succeeded; RHS = PR_Unauthorized.
property G30_ProvisionRequiresAuth
    fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult keyIsActive authResult =
  isKeyVaultResult vaultResult ==>
    isAuthResult authResult ==>
      provisionKey fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult keyIsActive
        == secureProvisionKey fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult
                              keyIsActive authResult

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// G31 — Provision response code is an active-key oracle.
//
// Two `provisionKey` invocations that agree on every input *except*
// `keyIsActive` return different ProvisionResults: `Succeeded` (200)
// when no active key, `Unauthorized` (401) when active. An attacker
// with no credentials can poll POST /enroll/key and read the status
// code to learn whether the device has an active enrollment key. This
// is a fingerprinting / state-disclosure oracle on a public-by-design
// endpoint.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

// EXPECTED VERDICT: FAIL.
// Counterexample: LHS = PR_Succeeded, RHS = PR_Unauthorized.
property G31_ProvisionResponseLeaksActiveState
    fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult =
  isKeyVaultResult vaultResult ==>
    fleetEnabled ==>
      validRequest ==>
        vaultResult == KV_Ok ==>
          provisionKey fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult False
            == provisionKey fleetEnabled validRequest vaultResult True

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// G32 — getStatus discloses state to unauthenticated callers.
//
// `getStatus(fleetEnabled, hasKey, keyIsActive, keyId)` has NO auth
// input. The C++ `handle_status` matches: anyone hitting GET
// /enroll/status learns fleetMode, hasKey, isActive, and the keyId
// (when active). For an enrolled device this leaks the active key
// identifier to an arbitrary network peer, which is a precondition
// for several other attacks (G33's keyId-probing oracle, log
// correlation across compromised peers, etc).
//
// `secureGetStatus` below adds a `callerAuth` input and returns an
// all-redacted EnrollmentStatus to unauthenticated callers. G32
// compares the two; Z3 finds a cex whenever the device actually has
// a key (i.e. `hasKey \/ keyIsActive`).
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

redactedStatus : EnrollmentStatus
redactedStatus =
  { fleetMode = FM_Disabled
  , hasKey    = False
  , keyId     = none
  , isActive  = False
  }

secureGetStatus : Bit -> Bit -> Bit -> UUID -> Bit -> EnrollmentStatus
secureGetStatus fleetEnabled hasKey keyIsActive keyId callerAuth =
  if callerAuth then getStatus fleetEnabled hasKey keyIsActive keyId
                else redactedStatus

// EXPECTED VERDICT: FAIL.
// Counterexample: any input where the produced status is not all-zero
// (e.g. fleetEnabled = True, or hasKey = True, or keyIsActive = True).
property G32_StatusLeaksToUnauthenticated
    fleetEnabled hasKey keyIsActive keyId =
  getStatus fleetEnabled hasKey keyIsActive keyId
    == secureGetStatus fleetEnabled hasKey keyIsActive keyId False

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// G33 — Activation response code is a keyId oracle.
//
// Inside `enrollDevice(true, true, _, _)`:
//
//   AR_Authenticated  + AC_IoFailure  -> ER_InternalError   (HTTP 500)
//   AR_Unauthenticated + (any)        -> ER_Unauthorized    (HTTP 401)
//
// An attacker who has obtained a single valid signed canonical
// payload (e.g. by passive observation on the link-local network
// before the canonicalizer's auth-header exclusion was deployed, or
// from a leaked log) can replay it against /enroll/key/{keyId}/activate
// for various candidate keyIds:
//
//   - 500 ("InternalError") => signature verified, keyId mismatched
//                              => the keyId is NOT the active one,
//                                 BUT the controller now leaks that
//                                 the request reached the activate
//                                 path with a valid HMAC.
//   - 401 ("Unauthorized")  => signature did not verify or auth
//                              short-circuited before lookup.
//
// The 500/401 split therefore acts as a "did your signature verify?"
// oracle on the network. A correct implementation would collapse the
// post-auth lookup failure to Unauthorized so the two cases are
// indistinguishable to the network observer.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

// EXPECTED VERDICT: FAIL.
// Counterexample: LHS = ER_InternalError, RHS = ER_Unauthorized.
property G33_ActivationOracleViaResponseCode =
  enrollDevice True True AR_Authenticated   AC_IoFailure
    == enrollDevice True True AR_Unauthenticated AC_Success

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// G34 — Claims are checked by name, not by value.
//
// `isValidClaims` in cpp/src/auth.cpp walks the JSON object and
// returns true iff every required claim NAME appears as a top-level
// key. It does not compare claim values. An attacker who can mint a
// signed request — for instance, the link-local provisioning attacker
// from G30 — can place arbitrary values under the required names
// (e.g. `{"device-id": "attacker", "tenant": "victim-tenant"}`) and
// pass the claims check intended to bind the signature to a specific
// device or tenant.
//
// Below: a bounded (2 claims, 2-byte names/values) model of the two
// checks. `claimsValidByName` is what the implementation does today;
// `claimsValidByPair` is the value-binding check it should do. G34
// asserts they agree; Z3 exhibits a cex with matching names and
// mismatched values.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

type ClaimW   = 2                                  // bytes per name / value
type ClaimsN  = 2                                  // bounded claim count
type ClaimNV  = ([ClaimW][8], [ClaimW][8])         // (name, value)

claimHasName : [ClaimW][8] -> [ClaimsN]ClaimNV -> Bit
claimHasName n actual = or [ a.0 == n | a <- actual ]

claimHasPair : ClaimNV -> [ClaimsN]ClaimNV -> Bit
claimHasPair p actual = or [ a == p | a <- actual ]

// What auth.cpp::isValidClaims actually does.
claimsValidByName : [ClaimsN]ClaimNV -> [ClaimsN]ClaimNV -> Bit
claimsValidByName required actual =
  and [ claimHasName r.0 actual | r <- required ]

// What it should do to bind the signature to claim VALUES.
claimsValidByPair : [ClaimsN]ClaimNV -> [ClaimsN]ClaimNV -> Bit
claimsValidByPair required actual =
  and [ claimHasPair r actual | r <- required ]

// EXPECTED VERDICT: FAIL.
// Counterexample: required = [(n, v1), ...], actual = [(n, v2), ...]
// with v1 != v2. LHS = True (name n present), RHS = False (pair (n,v1)
// absent).
property G34_ClaimsValueGap
    (required : [ClaimsN]ClaimNV) (actual : [ClaimsN]ClaimNV) =
  claimsValidByName required actual == claimsValidByPair required actual

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// G35 — HMAC accepts a zero / low-entropy key.
//
// Nothing in `provisionKey`, the key vault layer, or the abstract
// `hmacSha256` model constrains the key's entropy. A vault bug or
// misconfiguration that returned `secret = 0...0` would still pass
// every signature check that uses it: `isValidSignature 0 r
// (hmacSha256 0 r) == True` holds because P8 is parametric in the
// key. A defence-in-depth check at provision time (reject all-zero
// keys, enforce a min-entropy threshold) would block this; today the
// spec does not require it.
//
// Note: this property uses the SAME abstract HMAC algebra as P8/P9,
// so the cex is purely structural — it shows that the spec admits
// the degenerate key. A real countermeasure must live above the
// Cryptol model.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

// EXPECTED VERDICT: FAIL.
// Counterexample: any r — LHS = True (zero key verifies its own tag),
// RHS = False (the obligation the spec should enforce).
property G35_HmacZeroKeyAccepted (r : Request) =
  isValidSignature (zero : HmacKey) r (hmacSha256 (zero : HmacKey) r)
    == False
